const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Discovery and intelligence","serviceType":"Research service","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/product-development/","description":"Compound and ingredient discovery, patent landscape, efficacy and safety profiling, claims substantiation and regulatory intelligence.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How early should this work start?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Before formulation, and ideally at concept. The patent position and the claim position both constrain what the product can be, and both are cheap to establish now.\n\nThe most common sequence we see is formulation first, patent search second, and the discovery that the combination is covered third. Reversing the first two costs a few weeks and saves the programme."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is this a freedom-to-operate opinion?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. It is an intelligence assessment: the landscape, the live families, the claims they carry and where the exposure is, so you can make a commercial decision.\n\nA legal FTO opinion comes from a patent attorney. What we produce is what the attorney would otherwise spend billable hours assembling, which is why the two work well in sequence."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you decide what claims our evidence supports?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By mapping the evidence against the claim categories each target jurisdiction operates — structure-function under DSHEA, Article 13.1 and 14 health claims under EFSA, permitted lists under FSSAI, cosmetic versus drug boundaries under FDA and EC 1223/2009.\n\nThe answer is almost never the same across markets. A claims matrix stating what stands where is more useful than a single verdict, because the packaging and the dossier differ by market anyway."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What tools and databases do you use?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"PubMed, Embase, Scopus and Web of Science for literature; ClinicalTrials.gov and EUCTR for trials; chemical and patent databases for IP; and the regulator’s own ingredient and claim registers for compliance.\n\nMethod matters more than tooling: bibliometric and citation analysis, keyword and synonym expansion, structure searching, and systematic review technique applied to a commercial question rather than an academic one."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you find the gap in a crowded therapeutic area?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By mapping what has been studied against what has been studied well — which are different questions. Crowded fields frequently have large evidence gaps hidden behind a high publication count.\n\nLandscape mapping combines recent publications, citation patterns, trial registries and competitor claim sets to show where the unmet need and the absent evidence overlap. That overlap is the gap worth pursuing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you work on devices and combination products?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes — classification and predicate analysis, material compatibility, biocompatibility literature review against ISO 10993, and device-drug interaction intelligence for combination products.\n\nDevice work is governed by FDA 21 CFR 820, EU MDR 2017/745, ISO 14971 and, where software is involved, IEC 62304. Which apply depends on the classification, which is why that comes first."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you run laboratory or clinical studies?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. This is secondary research and intelligence work — the evidence that already exists, assembled and assessed.\n\nWhere the report concludes that the evidence is insufficient, it names the study that would settle it and what that study would need to show. Running it is a separate matter, and study design support is available for it."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Research services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Research services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/product-development/"}]}];

